### 3. HIGH — Empty `--corpus=` resolves to cwd and can return wrong candidates with exit 0

`Path("")` is `PosixPath('.')`. `--corpus=` therefore searches the process cwd. If cwd happens to contain `*.jsonl`, the tool exits 0 and prints candidates from the **wrong** corpus — wrong result reported as success.

**Command:**
```bash
ISOL=$(mktemp -d)
mkdir -p "$ISOL/cwd" "$ISOL/intended"
echo '{"id":"cwd-hit","instance":"secret cwd corpus match token ZZZCWD"}' >"$ISOL/cwd/x.jsonl"
echo '{"id":"intended-hit","instance":"secret intended corpus match token ZZZINT"}' >"$ISOL/intended/y.jsonl"
cd "$ISOL/cwd"
python3 /home/psimmons/projects/hindsight/.dispatch/worktrees/QA-panel-grok/bin/similar-causes.py --corpus= "secret match token"
echo exit=$?
cd - >/dev/null
rm -rf "$ISOL"
```

**Actual output:**
```
Candidates for human review. This tool does not determine duplication.
0.500 | cwd-hit | source: x.jsonl | secret cwd corpus match token ZZZCWD
exit=0
```
(No `intended-hit`; empty path was not rejected.)

